  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Johnny Carson studied the mechanics of laughter before becoming a national television personality. At the University of Nebraska, his senior thesis was a forty-five-minute recording explaining how to write comedy for radio. He studied radio and speech, with a minor in physics, and made an early television appearance playing a milkman in a university documentary.\n\nAfter graduation, Omaha station WOW employed him in radio and television. There he created The Squirrel’s Nest, a free-form morning variety programme. He carried that approach to Los Angeles with Carson’s Cellar, then reached a national audience through the game show Who Do You Trust? It was on that programme that he met announcer Ed McMahon.",
    "html": "<p>Johnny Carson studied the mechanics of laughter before becoming a national television personality. At the University of Nebraska, his senior thesis was a forty-five-minute recording explaining how to write comedy for radio. He studied radio and speech, with a minor in physics, and made an early television appearance playing a milkman in a university documentary.</p>\r\n<p>After graduation, Omaha station WOW employed him in radio and television. There he created The Squirrel’s Nest, a free-form morning variety programme. He carried that approach to Los Angeles with Carson’s Cellar, then reached a national audience through the game show Who Do You Trust? It was on that programme that he met announcer Ed McMahon.</p>\r\n",
